limit of 71 kg, up from the 64 kg at the Tokyo Games, and the 63 kg that had been contested from 2000 to 2016. " [:a {:href "/athletes/141480"} "Maude Charron"] " of Canada was the middleweight champion from the Tokyo Games, but competed in the lightweight in Paris where she won silver. Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/130714"} "Giorgia Bordignon"] ", who had won silver in Tokyo, did not compete in Paris, but the bronze medalist " [:a {:href "/athletes/146848"} "Chen Wen-Huei"] " (TPE) did return to the Olympics."] "\n\n" [:p {} "With the weight classes being changed slightly for the Paris Games, there was no clear favorite for medals. Despite this, the field featured a strong line-up of competitors, including the snatch world record holder " [:a {:href "/athletes/142257"} "Angie Palacios"] " (ECU), the Pan American Games champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/155589"} "Olivia Reeves"] " (USA), and the two-time European champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/155558"} "Marie Josèphe Fegue"] " (FRA)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Reeves led the way in the snatch when she lifted 117 kg with her third and final attempt. She was closely followed by Palacios and " [:a {:href "/athletes/155582"} "Loredana Toma"] " (ROU). The Filipino lifter " [:a {:href "/athletes/155579"} "Vanessa Sarno"] " was the first athlete to be eliminated after she failed to produce a valid attempt with her three lifts. Reeves continued her performance with her strong form in the clean &amp; jerk, lifting 145 kg to maintain her lead in the competition. Toma, who was in third place following the snatch, failed to record a valid lift, resulting in her elimination."] "\n\n" [:p {} "With a total of 262 kg, Reeves won gold for the United States, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/155548"} "Mari Sánchez"] " (COL) winning silver, and Palacios winning bronze. Chen Wen-Huei of Chinese Taipei, who had won bronze in Tokyo, could only manage sixth place in Paris."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"